Avida   http://avida.devosoft.org/
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Digital Evolution Laboratory at Michigan State University
(http://devolab.msu.edu)

Avida is a platform for experiments with digital evolution: self-replicating
programs that mutate and compete in a world you configure.

This repository holds the Rust port of Avida in rust/avida-native. It replaces
the C++ core, which was removed from the tree; the last commit that contains it
is the git tag cpp-final.


I.   REQUIREMENTS
II.  BUILDING
III. RUNNING
IV.  TESTS
V.   DOCUMENTATION


I. REQUIREMENTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  - A stable Rust toolchain (https://rustup.rs/)
  - For the desktop application on Linux: the usual windowing development
    packages (libxkbcommon, Wayland or X11, GTK 3)


II. BUILDING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  $ cd rust/avida-native
  $ cargo build --release -p avida          # the command-line program
  $ cargo build --release -p avida-tui      # the terminal interface
  $ cargo build --release -p avida-gui      # the desktop application

The programs are written to rust/avida-native/target/release. On macOS,
crates/gui/scripts/make-app-bundle.sh wraps the desktop application in
Avida.app.


III. RUNNING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Avida reads avida.cfg, the environment, the events and the instruction set from
the current directory. A complete default configuration is in
avida-core/support/config:

  $ cp -r avida-core/support/config my-run
  $ cd my-run
  $ ../rust/avida-native/target/release/avida           # population run
  $ ../rust/avida-native/target/release/avida -a        # analyze mode
  $ ../rust/avida-native/target/release/avida-tui .     # watch it in a terminal

Results are written to the data directory inside it. `avida -h` lists the
command-line options.


IV. TESTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  $ cd rust/avida-native
  $ cargo test --workspace

The consistency tests of the original program are kept in avida-core/tests with
their expected output; crates/avida/tests/consistency.rs runs the port against
them. tools/run_suite.py compares the port with a C++ reference binary on the
whole suite, byte for byte; it needs a binary built from the tag cpp-final.


V. DOCUMENTATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The user documentation is in docs/ (start at docs/index.md) and is published at
https://gedejong.github.io/avida-rs/. It covers installation, a first run, the
three front ends, the concepts, a reference of every setting and action, and
worked tutorials.

  docs/                               user documentation
  rust/avida-native/ARCHITECTURE.md   crates, layering, status, what is left
  rust/avida-native/PORTING.md        how parity with the C++ is kept
  documentation/                      the wiki of the C++ program (git submodule),
                                      kept until docs/ has taken over all of it;
                                      docs/migration/wiki-map.md says what moved where

